<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wanderer above the Sea of Fog</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1818</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Wanderer über dem Nebelmeer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wanderer above the Sea of Fog is a painting of about 1818 by the German Romantic artist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspar-david-friedrich">Caspar David Friedrich</a>. A man seen from behind stands on a rocky precipice above a sea of fog pierced by ridges, trees and mountains, a landscape Friedrich composed in the studio from sketches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> Sandstone Mountains. It has been interpreted as an emblem of self-reflection, as an evocation of the sublime and as an expression of his German liberal and nationalist feeling. It has been displayed at the Hamburger Kunsthalle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> since 1970.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wanderer-above-the-sea-of-fog.jpg" alt="Wanderer above the Sea of Fog" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Caspar David Friedrich · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich,_Caspar_David_-_Wanderer_%C3%BCber_dem_Nebelmeer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspar-david-friedrich">Caspar David Friedrich</a> painted Wanderer above the Sea of Fog in 1818, though some sources indicate 1817. Its provenance in the 19th century is unclear. By 1939 it belonged to the gallery of Wilhelm August Luz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and it was then apparently sold to the German lawyer Ernst Henke before returning to the Luz gallery. After passing between private collections it was acquired by the Hamburger Kunsthalle in 1970. In 1817 Friedrich had struck up a friendship with the scientist Carl Gustav Carus and the Norwegian painter Johan Christian Dahl, and in January 1818 he married Caroline Brommer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A man in a dark green overcoat stands on a rocky precipice with his back to the viewer, gripping a walking stick in his right hand, his hair caught in the wind. Ridges and forested escarpments jut from the sea of fog in the middle ground, and faded mountains rise on the left and level into lowland plains on the right. The landscape is composed of elements of the Elbe Sandstone Mountains in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, sketched in the field and rearranged by Friedrich in the studio. The Zirkelstein stands in the background on the right, and the rocks under the traveller are a group on the Kaiserkrone.</p><p>Friedrich used the Rückenfigur, a figure turned away from the viewer, considerably more often than other artists, and it is generally thought to invite viewers inside the painting. Helmut Börsch-Supan wrote that it is harder to imagine this landscape without a figure than in any other painting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Some suggest the man is a self-portrait, pointing to his red hair, and the painting has been read as an emblem of self-reflection and as an evocation of the sublime. Friedrich wrote that the artist should paint both what he has in front of him and what he sees inside himself. He was an outspoken supporter of German liberal and nationalist feeling, and the art historian Norbert Wolf holds that the figure wears an Altdeutsch outfit, a political statement in that era; other scholars describe the clothing as a Jäger infantry uniform.</p><p>The painting and Friedrich's work were not at first regarded as masterpieces, though he was respected in German and Russian circles. His reputation improved in the 20th century, and the painting has since appeared on book covers and other objects. Robert Macfarlane calls it the archetypical image of the mountain-climbing visionary.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wanderer Above the Sea of Fog” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Painters turned to drama and landscape: Friedrich’s <b>Wanderer above the Sea of Fog</b> of about 1818, Théodore Géricault’s The Raft of the Medusa of 1819, the skies of J. M. W. Turner and John Constable.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
